<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-confidence-intervals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Confidence Intervals</title>
  <p> 
    Over the last few sections, we've built up a process of hypothesis
    testing. In that process, we've defined a bunch of new tools. I
    now want to apply those tools to a similar, parallel process: the
    creation of confidence intervals. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Think, again, on the question of hypothesis testing. We have a
    sample and, from it, a sample statistic. We want to know what this
    sample statistic means. We want to understand what it says about
    a population parameter. So we made a hypothesis and tested it.
    This is a good approach, but not the only approach.
  </p>
  <p>
    Confidence intervals start from the same place: we have a sample
    and a sample statistic and we want to know about the population
    parameter. But now, instead of using hypothesis, we take a
    different approach. Instead, I want to ask, mathematically, how
    confidence I am in the sample statistic. How likely is it to be
    close to the population paramater? Could I make a range around the
    sample statistic in which the population parameter might fall? If
    I can, such a range is called a confidence interval.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before starting with the definitions, let me use a very common
    example: opinion polls. Nanos Research did poll in October 2026
    with a sample of 1014 Canadian asking about their preferred
    federal political party. The results of this poll were <m>45\%</m>
    for the Liberal party and  <m>29\%</m> for the Conservative party, and
    other values for the remaining parties. When they reported this
    poll, it came with this caveat: the poll is expected to be
    correct within plus of minus 3.1 percent, 19 times out of 20. If
    you haven't ever interacted with opinion polls before, you've
    likely heard similar things. 
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a confidence interval. It says that these pollsters are
    confidence that their numbers will be within <m>3.1\%</m> of the
    actually population most of the time, with <m>19</m> times out of
    <m>20</m> being their measure of most of the time. <m>19</m> out
    of <m>20</m> is <m>95\%</m>, so they are <m>95\%</m> confident.
    This is a <m>95%</m> confidence interval. In the actual population
    of all Canadian, the Liberal support should be between
    <m>41.9</m> and <m>48.1</m>. 
  </p>
  <p>
    This is all probability. Saying <m>19</m> times out of <m>20</m>
    means that if the same poll was run many times, these pollsters
    expect that the poll would fall within their error <m>95\%</m> of
    the time. 
  </p>
  <p>
    This is what we want to formalize in this section. How did they
    calculate this <m>\pm 3.1\%</m>? Why are the <m>95\%</m> confident
    in this number?
  </p>
  <p>
    I'll work through example and give the general structure along the
    way. The example here is a psychology experiment about two ways of
    asking a purchasing question. This is a controled trial: there is a
    control group and a treatment group, and each group has a
    percentage of buying and not buying. The topic is purchasing a DVD
    that, by assumption, all participants actually want to see. 
    (obviously, this is old data about purchasing physical media). The
    control group is asked a simple question: do you want to purchase
    the film? The treatment group was asked a slightly more
    complicated quesiton: do you want to purchase the film or save the
    money for something else later? The psychologists wanted to know if the
    additional <sq>save the money for something else later</sq>
    addition to the question made a difference. 
  </p>
  <table xml:id="table-dvd-purchase">
    <title>DVD Purchasing Data</title>
    <tabular>
      <row bottom="medium">>
      <cell> Group </cell>
      <cell> Buy </cell>
      <cell> Do Not Buy </cell>
      <cell> Total </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
      <cell> Control </cell>
      <cell> 56 </cell>
      <cell> 19 </cell>
      <cell> 75 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
      <cell> Treatment </cell>
      <cell> 41 </cell>
      <cell> 34 </cell>
      <cell> 75 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
      <cell> Total </cell>
      <cell> 97 </cell>
      <cell> 53 </cell>
      <cell> 150 </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <p>
    <xref ref="table-dvd-purchase"/> is the data for this study. Not
    that there are 150 subject, 75 in each group, since we will need
    that data for later. At a glance, more people in the treatment
    group chose not to purchase. So, at least in the sample, the
    different question had an effect. 
  </p>
  <p>
    To find a confidence interval, I start the same way: with the
    sample statistic. The sample statistic is a different
    inproportion: the number of people in the treatment group who
    chose not to purchase minute the number of people in the control
    group who chose not to purchse. From the table, I can calculate
    this. 
    <md>
      \hat{p}_T - \hat{p}_C = \frac{34}{75} - \frac{19}{75} = 0.453 -
      0.253 = 0.200
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    A hypothesis test would wonter if this sample statistic was
    significant. Now, instead, we want a confidence interval. What
    does that mean? It means that around this <m>20</m> percentage points
    difference, we want to know where the population parameter
    actually lies.
    That population parameter is stil a difference in proportions, but
    it is unknown. We think this reminder of opportunity cost does affect
    people's decision making, but we don't know by how much. The
    sample statistic gives us a starting point. We make a range aroung 
    the sample statistic: a confidence range. It might be pretty
    close: we might be 95% confident that the population parameter is
    between 0.190 and 0.210. If might be very precise, between 0.199
    and 0.201. If might be less precise, between 0.150 and 0.250. It
    might be quite wide, between 0 and 0.4, including the possibility
    that there is no diffrence at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    I could run a randomization process here, as we did with
    hypothesis testing. But now that we've built up all this theory of
    the normal distribution and <m>z</m>-score, it makes sense to use
    that. Therefore, I'm going to make a new sampling distribution. 
    The central limit theorem will tell me that, under certain
    reasonable condition, the sampling distribution is normal. This
    makes all the mathematic here work: we can work with a normal
    distribution and its properties. 
  </p>
  <p>
    For the sampling distribution, I need the mean and the standard
    deviation. Let me start with the mean. In hypothesis testing for a
    difference in proportions, the mean was zero. This was from the
    null hypothesis, which claims no difference at all. However, now I
    don't have a null hypothesis. So what is the mean? Well, for
    confidence intervals, the mean is set to be exactly the sample
    statistic. I want a distribution of random samples centred on my
    sample statistic. This is the first big difference: how to set the
    mean. In this example, the mean is <m>\mu = 0.200</m>, since that
    was the sample statistic. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Then I need the standard error, the standard deviation of this
    sampling distribution. Let me first fill in a detail I omited from
    previous videos: how to calculate this for hypothesis testing. If
    I was doing hypothesis testing for a different in proportion, I
    calculate the stadard in several step. First I calculate the total
    success proprotion, also called the <sq>pooled proportion</sq> in
    some texts. This is the total number of successes divided by the
    total number of cases. This study is counting <sq>not
    purchasing</sq> as a success. From the table, here is the
    calculation.
    <md>
      P = \frac{19 + 34}{75 + 75} = \frac{53}{150} = 0.3533
    </md>
    Then if <m>n_1</m> and <m>n_2</m> are the sizes of each of the
    groups being compared (in this case, treatment and control), then
    the following calculations gives the sandard error. 
    <md>
      SE = \sqrt{P(1-P)\left( \frac{1}{n_1} + \frac{1}{n_2} \right) }
    </md>
    I can plug the numbers into this example. 
    <md>
      SE = \sqrt{0.3533(1-0.3533)\left( \frac{1}{75} + \frac{1}{75}
      \right)} = 0.0781
    </md>
    This is the sandard error I would use for hypothesis testing. 
  </p>
  <p>
    For confidence intervals, due to some subtleties in the
    mathematical modelling, there is a different standard error. There
    is no need to calculate the proportion of total successes.
    Instead, using <m>p_1</m> as the proportion of success in the
    treatment group and <m>p_2</m> as the propostion of successes in
    the control group, this is the formula for standard error. 
    <md>
      SE = \sqrt{ \frac{p_1(1-p_1)}{n_1} + \frac{p_2(1-p_2)}{n_2}}
    </md>
    This is pretty similar to the previouis formula. The only
    different is that the two cases are kept separate and not pooled
    together.  In this case <m>p_1 = \frac{34}{75} = 0.453</m> and <m>
    p_2 = \frac{19}{75} = 0.253</m>. I can then put all the number in
    and calculate. 
    <md>
      SE = \sqrt{ \frac{0.453(1-0.453)}{75} +
      \frac{0.253(1-0.253)}{75}} = 0.0763
    </md>
    Now I have the standard errror. Since I know a mean and a standard
    error, I know the sampling distribution entirely. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Now how do I use this? Well, let me things about the normal
    distribution again. My sample is in the middle of the normal
    distribution, and these randomly generated samples sptread out
    from the normal distribution. The big idea is this: the
    probability of the population parameter actually being in some
    range is given by the area under the curve of this sampling
    distribution. Again, areas are probabilities. Now say I want a
    <m>95\%</m> confidence. That means I want <m>95\%</m> of the area.
    <xref ref="figure-confidence-area"/> shows this: I've drawn a box
    beneath with exactly <m>95\%</m> of the area of the normal
    distribution falls. 
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-confidence-area">
    <caption>Area for a Confidence Interval</caption>
    <image width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
              ytick=\empty,
            ]
            \addplot [
              domain=-3:3,
              samples=200,
            ]
            {exp(-x^2 / (2)) / (sqrt(2*pi))};
            \draw (-2,0) -- (-2,0.4) -- (2,0.4) -- (2,0);
          \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    I want a range: remember that confidence intervals are a range
    around the sample statistic. So how far out did I actually have to
    go to get this <m>95\%</m> area? It turns out I had to go out
    <m>1.96</m> standard deviations. Much like <m>z</m>-scores, I use
    standard deviations as the units, since they will always work for
    any normal distribution.
  </p>
  <p>
    This lets met finish the calculation. I know my mean, which is
    <m>0.200</m>. I know my standard deviation, which is <m>0.076</m>.
    I calculate the mean minus <m>1.96</m> standard deviations.
    <md>
      0.200 - (1.96)(0.076) = 0.051
    </md>
    Then I calculate the mean plut <m>1.96</m> standard deviations.
    <md>
      0.200 + (1.96)(0.076) = 0.349
    </md>
    Then I have my interval. In this situation, I can be <m>95\%</m>
    confident that the population parameter falls in the interval
    <m>[0.051,0.349]</m>. Of note, this interval does not include
    zero, so in particular, I can be <m>95\%</m> confident that my
    samples actually shows some real difference in the population.
    <xref ref="figure-confidence-area2"/> shows the specific normal
    distribution for this situation with its confidence interval. 
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-confidence-area2">
    <caption>Area for the Example Confidence Interval</caption>
    <image width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
              ytick=\empty,
            ]
            \addplot [
              domain=0:0.4,
              samples=200,
            ]
            {exp(-(x-0.2)^2 / (2*((0.076)^2))) / (sqrt(2*pi*((0.076)^2)))};
            \draw (0.051,0) -- (0.051,5.25) -- (0.349,5.25) -- (0.349,0);
          \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Let me recap the process
    <ul>
      <li>
        We have a sample statistic. 
      </li>
      <li>
        That sample statistic becomes the mean of the sampling
        distribution. 
      </li>
      <li>
        The central limit theorem says that the sampling distribution
        is normal (again, under reasonable conditions). 
      </li>
      <li>
        We calculate the standard deviation, also called standard
        error, using the formula given in this section.
      </li>
      <li>
        Then, for each standard of confidence, there is a confidence
        interval defined in terms of standard deviations. In this
        case, it was <m>1.96</m> standard deviation.
      </li>
      <li>
        We use <m>1.96</m>, the mean, and standard error to calculate
        the confidence interval. 
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</section>

